WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.380
<v Instructor>Now let's talk about</v>

2
00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.300
the more important topic for you to remember,

3
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.920
which is CloudWatch Logs.

4
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.510
CloudWatch Logs is going to be very important

5
00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.220
going into the SA Pro exam.

6
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.740
And so first you need to know how to get data

7
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.120
into CloudWatch Logs.

8
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.500
So the first way to get data

9
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.620
is through the SDK, or the CloudWatch Logs Agent,

10
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.570
or the CloudWatch Unified Agent.

11
00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.520
I will show you the difference between the Logs Agent

12
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.700
and the Unified Agent in a slide in this lecture.

13
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:26.580
Now we can also get data in CloudWatch Logs

14
00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:30.240
through some integration with AWS services.

15
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.090
So for example, Elastic Beanstalk can help you

16
00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.310
collect all the logs from your application

17
00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.040
and send that to CloudWatch Logs directly.

18
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.070
ECS also has a log driver to collect log

19
00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:44.160
from the containers and send that into CloudWatch Logs.

20
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.860
Lambda has a direct integration with CloudWatch Logs

21
00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.870
and will give you all the function logs.

22
00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.300
The VPC Flow Logs can be sent to CloudWatch.

23
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.457
API Gateway Access Logs,

24
00:00:53.457 --> 00:00:56.610
and CloudTrail based on the filter you wanna set.

25
00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.890
So good to know the CloudWatch Logs Agent

26
00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.900
can be installed on EC2 machines,

27
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.290
but not only it can be installed on your on-premise Vms

28
00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.180
or on any server you want

29
00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.190
as long as it has logs and you wanna send them

30
00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:09.510
into CloudWatch Logs.

31
00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.360
Finally, you can also get DNS queries logged

32
00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.420
from Route53 into CloudWatch Logs.

33
00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.320
Okay, so next, how does CloudWatch Logs work in details?

34
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.680
So we have log groups, and you can give any name you want

35
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.720
that usually represents an application.

36
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:25.950
And within your log groups

37
00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:27.480
you're going to have log streams

38
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.150
which are going to be instances within your application

39
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.190
of the log files or your containers.

40
00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:34.650
So it's up to you to organize this the way you want,

41
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:37.140
and usually it doesn't really matter.

42
00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.150
What matters is what inside log stream is,

43
00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.740
which is your log itself.

44
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.200
You can define log expiration policies,

45
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.660
so your log can be chosen to never expire,

46
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.150
or expire after 30 days, et cetera, et cetera.

47
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:49.590
And if you do expire your logs,

48
00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:51.060
obviously, you need to make sure

49
00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.890
that they're going to be persisted somewhere

50
00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:55.740
in case you need to analyze them in the future.

51
00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.000
So it is possible

52
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.730
for you to encrypt your CloudWatch Logs with KMS.

53
00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.370
And CloudWatch Logs has the possibility to send these logs

54
00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.860
to other destinations.

55
00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.210
So we'll see this in a second

56
00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:09.360
where we have Amazon S3 where we can create exports.

57
00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.980
We have Kinesis Data Stream

58
00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:13.020
if you wanted to stream the CloudWatch Logs into something

59
00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.770
that gives you more ability to analyze them in real time.

60
00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:17.820
Kinesis Data Firehose,

61
00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.380
again, if you want to export these logs into S3,

62
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.370
ElasticSearch, Redshift, or Splunk.

63
00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:27.450
And Lambda and ElasticSearch through a Lambda integration.

64
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.250
We'll go over those in a second.

65
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.020
But now let's talk about CloudWatch Logs

66
00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.940
Metric Filters and Insights.

67
00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.820
So CloudWatch Logs can use a filter expression,

68
00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.480
and these filter expression can result in creating a metric,

69
00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.420
hence the name Logs Metric Filter.

70
00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.250
So for example, say you are looking

71
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.200
for a specific IP inside of a log

72
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.720
and you want to have an alarm or a metric at least

73
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.120
based on how many times that IP has been found,

74
00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.550
then you would create a metric filter.

75
00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:56.310
Or if you wanted to count the number of occurrences

76
00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.370
of the word error in your logs, for example,

77
00:02:58.370 --> 00:02:59.610
if you wanted to get alerted

78
00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.790
in case your application goes crazy and has too many errors,

79
00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:04.230
then that could be a very good use case

80
00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.060
for CloudWatch Logs Metric Filter as well.

81
00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.400
And these metric filters can be used

82
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.050
to trigger CloudWatch alarms,

83
00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.720
and from this you have all your automations you need.

84
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.760
And if you wanted to analyze your logs

85
00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.530
within CloudWatch Logs,

86
00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.440
you could use something called CloudWatch Logs Insights

87
00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.800
to query logs directly, and even keep these queries

88
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.200
and add them to a CloudWatch Dashboard.

89
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.910
So the cool thing about CloudWatch Logs Insights

90
00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.070
is that it has also simple queries

91
00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.350
of what you can do directly from within CloudWatch.

92
00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:34.050
For example, you can have the number of exceptions

93
00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:35.670
logged every five minutes,

94
00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:38.340
or the list of log events that are not exceptions.

95
00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.370
Or something specific to Lambda or Route53

96
00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.770
or VPC Flow Logs, et cetera, et cetera.

97
00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:47.130
Okay, so say you wanted to not analyze your logs

98
00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:48.090
within CloudWatch Logs,

99
00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.160
but you wanted to analyze them with your own tools.

100
00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:50.993
And so for this

101
00:03:50.993 --> 00:03:53.940
you need to export the data of CloudWatch Logs.

102
00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:57.360
So the first thing we have to know is the S3 export.

103
00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.820
Very simple, we take the CloudWatch Logs

104
00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.190
and we send it into Amazon S3.

105
00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.590
There is a feature directly made for that.

106
00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:07.350
And to send these logs to Amazon S3 you must encrypt them.

107
00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:11.069
And so this feature supports SSE-S3

108
00:04:11.069 --> 00:04:15.120
and SSE-KMS as encryption mechanisms.

109
00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.660
On top of it, log data can take up to 12 hours

110
00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:20.790
to become available for an export.

111
00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.310
So this is definitely not real time,

112
00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:24.930
and this is not automated.

113
00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.970
You have to use the API call named CreateExportTask

114
00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.810
to get the data from CloudWatch Logs into Amazon S3.

115
00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.160
So it's not near-real time,

116
00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.300
it's not real time.

117
00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.303
For this, we can use CloudWatch Logs Subscriptions instead.

118
00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.350
So let's talk about CloudWatch Logs Subscription now.

119
00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:46.320
So your CloudWatch Logs can get a subscription filter

120
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.120
and you can filter the type of logs you want

121
00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.410
out of CloudWatch Logs.

122
00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.200
And this subscription filter can send data

123
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.480
to many different outputs.

124
00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.020
So the first one is a managed Lambda function by AWS,

125
00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.750
and that Lambda function is meant to send data

126
00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:03.750
to Amazon ElasticSearch in real time.

127
00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.520
But it's very possible for you

128
00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.410
to define your own Lambda function

129
00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:09.090
and do whatever you want in real time

130
00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:11.670
on top of the subscription filter.

131
00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:14.910
Then you have the option to also use a subscription filter

132
00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:17.130
with Kinesis Data Firehose.

133
00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.110
And we know Kinesis Data Firehose now,

134
00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:22.770
so we can send data near-real time into Amazon S3,

135
00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:25.140
or into Amazon ElasticSearch.

136
00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:28.320
So the question is, should we use Kinesis Data Firehose

137
00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.620
or Lambda to send data to Amazon ES or Amazon S3?

138
00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:33.270
Well, it really depends on your requirements.

139
00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:34.800
If you have the real time requirements,

140
00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.660
you have to use a Lambda function.

141
00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:39.030
But if you're willing to have one minute of lag,

142
00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:41.520
for example, and you are okay with near-real time,

143
00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:42.990
then Kinesis Data Firehose

144
00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.670
is going to be a better choice probably,

145
00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:48.360
a bit less expensive, and more scalable.

146
00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.970
Finally, it is possible for you to take these logs

147
00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:53.760
from the subscription filter into Kinesis Data Streams,

148
00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.250
and from it we can use whatever tool we would need.

149
00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.160
So Kinesis Data Firehose, Kinesis Data Analytics,

150
00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.470
EC2 instances that are running the KCL,

151
00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:02.520
or a Lambda function.

152
00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:06.120
Another good architecture to know with CloudWatch Logs

153
00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.550
is that you can do it for multi-account

154
00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:11.340
and multi-region Log Aggregation.

155
00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:13.620
So say you have an Account A, Region 1,

156
00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:14.820
you create the CloudWAtch Logs

157
00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:16.860
and a subscription filter.

158
00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:19.440
And then this sends it into a Kinesis Data Stream

159
00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.990
in a central logging account.

160
00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:23.100
Same for another account.

161
00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:24.750
So Account B, Region 2.

162
00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:26.730
We have the CloudWatch Logs, subscription filter,

163
00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:28.560
and send this into a Kinesis Data stream

164
00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.240
in the central logging account.

165
00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.520
And for account B I just showed that it has another region,

166
00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.160
so it could be multi-account and multi-region.

167
00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.890
And from this it has to be into Kinesis Data Stream.

168
00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:40.440
You can define your own Kinesis Data Firehose

169
00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:44.910
and sync that data near-real time into Amazon S3,

170
00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:47.760
and this will be into your central security

171
00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:48.900
or logging account.

172
00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:49.800
So this could be very good,

173
00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:52.920
and this is a very, very common architecture.

174
00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.170
Now you can use the CloudWatch Agent

175
00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:57.900
to actually get logs from your EC2 instances.

176
00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:00.990
And there is tight integration using the CloudWatch Agent

177
00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:03.030
and Systems Manager.

178
00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:05.550
So you can install the CloudWatch Agent, for example,

179
00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.590
using the SSM Run Command.

180
00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:09.390
So say we have an EC2 instance,

181
00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:11.280
it has the SSM agent running

182
00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.170
and it's set up with Systems Manager.

183
00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:17.129
Then using a Run Command called ConfigureAWSPackage,

184
00:07:17.129 --> 00:07:19.440
and the package is AmazonCloudWatchAgent,

185
00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:21.900
then the CloudWatch Agent will be automatically set up

186
00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:23.610
on your EC2 instance.

187
00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:25.410
And the alternative is, again,

188
00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:29.010
to use an EC2 instance with the SSM agent installed on it.

189
00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:30.900
But this time instead of Run Command

190
00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:34.440
you use the Systems Manager State Manager feature

191
00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.050
to make sure that, again, the CloudWatch Agent

192
00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:38.460
is installed on your EC2 instance,

193
00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:40.170
and it's two ways of doing it.

194
00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:44.010
And finally, if you don't have the SSM agent installed

195
00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:45.870
but you still want to use SSM,

196
00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:49.200
the EC2 instance can actually be installed

197
00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:50.640
with the CloudWatch agent itself.

198
00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:52.530
So you can install it with some commands.

199
00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:55.620
And then when you configure and start the CloudWatch agent

200
00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:58.110
you can instruct it to actually download

201
00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:00.420
the agent configuration directly

202
00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:02.550
from the SSM Parameter Store,

203
00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.580
therefore making the CloudWatch Agent very complete

204
00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:07.860
and configured out of the box.

205
00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:09.450
Okay, so that's it for this lecture.

206
00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:10.530
I hope you liked it,

207
00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:12.480
and I will see you in the next lecture.

